| AFV3       | 0      | 70    | +70   |  ← cjxl never picks

SECONDARY: F-heuristic (activity-based quant reduction) over-fires in
zenjxl by 3-21× on every shared strategy. On DCT16X16 X-channel:
cjxl 0.5% → zenjxl 10.6%. On DCT32X16 X: cjxl 0.0% → zenjxl 6.3%.

TERTIARY: On matched (bx, by, channel) blocks with matched strategy, zenjxl's
final new_quant is consistently LOWER than cjxl's (mean delta -0.34 to -0.90
across strategies). Lower quant → less coarse → more bits → higher quality.

=== INTERPRETATION ===

The +2 SSIM2 gap (cjxl ABOVE us) is NOT caused by AdjustQuantBlockAC firing
differently to OUR disadvantage. The opposite: our F-heuristic fires more
often and produces lower quant (= less coarse, more bits). On the matched
subset, zenjxl produces 67% of blocks with different (and uniformly
quality-favouring) quant values.

The structural divergence IS the AC strategy search: zenjxl picks PARTIAL
transforms (DCT2X2/4X4/4X8/8X4, IDENTITY, AFV0-3) for 472 first-blocks where
cjxl picks larger non-partial transforms. These partial picks NEVER go
through AdjustQuantBlockAC (libjxl early-returns at enc_group.cc:122-124).
On those blocks, zenjxl uses fixed thresholds {0.58, 0.62, 0.62, 0.62}
(matching libjxl's QuantizeBlockAC path for non-Y channels) and the original
unadjusted quant from the field — no per-block quality lift.

Mean orig_quant on partial-strategy zenjxl blocks (mean ~4.10 to ~5.24)
is COMPARABLE to non-partial blocks (~4.3 to ~5.1 for cjxl/zenjxl combined),
so the partial-strategy choice itself isn't dropping more bits — but it
removes the perceptually-informed F-heuristic from those blocks.

=== SPECIFIC LIBJXL CODE LOCATIONS ===

The "no AdjustQuantBlockAC for partial kinds" gate that diverges from
where zenjxl picks those kinds:
  ~/work/jxl-efforts/libjxl--sa-a/lib/jxl/enc_group.cc:112-124

  constexpr size_t kPartialBlockKinds =
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::IDENTITY)) |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::DCT2X2))   |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::DCT4X4))   |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::DCT4X8))   |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::DCT8X4))   |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::AFV0))     |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::AFV1))     |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::AFV2))     |
      (1 << static_cast<size_t>(AcStrategyType::AFV3));
  if ((1 << static_cast<size_t>(quant_kind)) & kPartialBlockKinds) {
    return;
  }

Our equivalent: jxl-encoder/src/vardct/quantize.rs:149-162 (RAW_STRATEGY_*
matches that early-return). The IMPLEMENTATION matches; the divergence is
that zenjxl's AC strategy SEARCH selects these kinds where cjxl doesn't.

=== FALSIFICATION CHECKS ===

Did the SSIM2 gap come from AdjustQuantBlockAC quant differences?  NO.
Our quant deltas on matched-strategy blocks would IMPROVE SSIM2 (lower
quant = more bits / less coarse). The +2 SSIM2 gap (cjxl above us) is in
the opposite direction from what AdjustQuantBlockAC differences predict.

Did the SSIM2 gap come from F-heuristic over-firing?  NO (same reason —
F reduces quant, which would help SSIM2 not hurt).

Did the SSIM2 gap come from cjxl using larger transforms on smooth photo
content?  POSSIBLE but NOT directly proven by this dump. The 472-block
allocation difference to partial transforms is a STRATEGY SEARCH issue,
not an Adjust issue. Filed as next-investigation candidate.

=== NEXT-INVESTIGATION CANDIDATES (NOT THIS CHUNK) ===

1. AC strategy search divergence: WHY does zenjxl allocate 79 DCT4X8 + 101
   DCT8X4 + 25 IDENTITY + 43 DCT2X2 + 328 AFV0-3 first-blocks on smooth
   photo content where cjxl picks larger transforms? Investigation should
   target `find_best_8x8_transform` and the AFV scoring path in
   `vardct/ac_strategy_search.rs` at e9 d=4.

2. Adaptive-quant pre-pass divergence: orig_quant means differ slightly
   (cjxl 4.3-5.1 vs zen 4.5-5.2 in the same brackets). Even before
   AdjustQuantBlockAC fires, the quant_field that feeds it is different.
   Investigation should compare adaptive_quant.rs::compute_quant_field
   output against libjxl ComputeQuantField pre-pass.

3. The +2 SSIM2 gap on cjxl SIDE is likely accumulated from MULTIPLE
   small-magnitude divergences upstream of AdjustQuantBlockAC. A pure
   AdjustQuantBlockAC chunk cannot close it.

=== DO NOT ===

- DO NOT spawn another AdjustQuantBlockAC chunk hoping to close the SSIM2
  gap. Measurement is conclusive: our Adjust diverges in the QUALITY-
  FAVOURING direction (lower quant on matched blocks).
- DO NOT remove the F-heuristic guard in zenjxl thinking it over-fires.
  It's libjxl-faithful (same code path); the firing-rate difference comes
  from different INPUT (quant_field, sum_of_vals, hf_nonzeros) caused by
  upstream strategy / adaptive-quant divergence, not by the F-heuristic
  itself.
- DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any of these divergences (per W44-66
  user correction). They're real structural-search and adaptive-quant
  upstream-pipeline divergences.
- DO NOT mark P5 (AdjustQuantBlockAC) as the next priority for AUDIT-8.
  Per the SA-A finding, the right next angles are AC strategy search
  divergence and adaptive_quant pre-pass divergence.

